         <h2>Every discharge, visible within minutes</h2>
         <p>Hanoi’s rivers receive large volumes of untreated wastewater every day. Only about 30% of the city’s domestic wastewater is treated (VnExpress, 2024), and factories and drains can discharge at night or in heavy rain, when nobody is watching. Inspections rely on hand-taken samples and laboratory tests that take days, so polluters are rarely traced and residents downstream are not warned.</p>
         <p>This report proposes Hanoi WaterWatch: small solar-powered sensor stations at high-risk drain outfalls that measure pH, dissolved oxygen, turbidity and temperature every few seconds. When readings stay outside national QCVN limits, the system alerts the environment authority, turns the outfall red on a public map app and warns nearby residents. An inspector then takes a laboratory sample at that exact outfall before any fine is issued.</p>
-        <p>The team chose this design over resident test kits and more frequent manual sampling because it is the only option that is both real-time and traceable to a single outfall. A simulated prototype, built without hardware, demonstrates the full chain from a polluted reading to a phone alert. A ten-station pilot would cost about US$6,100 in its first year, a tiny fraction of what Hanoi now spends restoring one river.</p>
+        <p>The team chose this design over resident test kits and more frequent manual sampling because it is the only option that is both real-time and traceable to a single outfall. A simulated prototype, built without hardware, demonstrates the full chain from a polluted reading to a phone alert. Under the team’s provisional assumptions, the ten-station, first-year scenario subtotal is <span data-hww-first-year-cost>US$6,105.70</span>. This is not a complete pilot budget: the final hardware, power compatibility, site work and unpriced services still require verification and quotations.</p>
